<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Josiah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">reigned c. 640–<a href="/years/609-bce">609 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Josiah was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> from about 640 to 609 BCE. According to the Bible, the discovery of a 'book of the law' in the Temple during his reign led him to carry out a sweeping religious reform, abolishing other cults and centralising worship in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. He was killed at Megiddo in 609 BCE in an encounter with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/necho-ii">Pharaoh Necho II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josiah.jpg" alt="Josiah" width="320" height="471" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This image was produced by me, David Castor (dcastor). The pictures I submit to the Wikipedia Project are released to the public domain. … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%85hus_kyrka-12.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hezekiah is credited with a first reform, and <b>Josiah</b> (c. 640–609 BCE), after a book of the law was found in the temple in 622 BCE, centralised worship in Jerusalem, destroyed local shrines and celebrated a great Passover.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…at first one of several cults in the land, and many scholars think the worship of one God alone, monotheism, took shape gradually, through the prophets who denounced the worship of other gods and through the reforms of King <b>Josiah</b> of Judah in the late 7th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Josiah</b>, son of chief Arapsang, explained the picture of Rodgers as part animal by the colonial government's mistreatment of the Kipsigis, who came to see its people as &quot;man-eaters&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/chemirocha">Chemirocha</a></li><li><blockquote>William <b>Josiah</b> Irons made an English version in 1849 that keeps the rhyme and metre; edited, it is approved for funerals in the Catholic ordinariates for former Anglicans.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dies-irae">Dies irae</a></li><li><blockquote>Necho II began a canal from the Nile to the Red Sea, sent a Phoenician fleet around Africa and killed King <b>Josiah</b> of Judah at Megiddo, but lost to Babylon at Carchemish in 605 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-sixth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-sixth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
